#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-09-17.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="080353"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#080353" label="[08:03:53]">&lt;daddy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">howdy o/ thought i was still in here, i was not.</span></div><div class="line" id="080442"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#080442" label="[08:04:42]">&lt;daddy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: are there any plans for a new stage0-posix tag soon? i'm trying to update live-bootstrap within stagex and it needs a newer stage0-posix for unxz that has yet to be released</span></div><div class="line" id="080922"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#080922" label="[08:09:22]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Can I help?</span></div><div class="line" id="152549"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#152549" label="[15:25:49]">&lt;Guest50&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hi, I found the stage0 project, which sounds very interesting</span></div><div class="line" id="152625"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#152625" label="[15:26:25]">&lt;Guest50&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The readme says, under &quot;Future dierctions&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="152625"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#152625" label="[15:26:25]">&lt;Guest50&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&gt; Hardware</span></div><div class="line" id="152626"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#152626" label="[15:26:26]">&lt;Guest50&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&gt; Implement the Knight processor in FPGA and then convert into TTL.</span></div><div class="line" id="152651"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#152651" label="[15:26:51]">&lt;Guest50&gt;</a></span><span class="message">What is meant by &quot;TTL&quot; here ?</span></div><div class="line" id="152730"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#152730" label="[15:27:30]">&lt;Guest50&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess it's not just the transistor-transistor logic technology, e.g. as opposed to CMOS</span></div><div class="line" id="160750"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#160750" label="[16:07:50]">&lt;Maja&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i assume shit like the 74xx logic series</span></div><div class="line" id="181457"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#181457" label="[18:14:57]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It needs to be something doable without modern lithography equipment that itself relies on firmware and modern lithography for its own existence</span></div><div class="line" id="181549"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#181549" label="[18:15:49]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Bootstrapping lithography would be a formidable challenge, mainly because virtually all relevant lithography techniques are proprietary and trade secret</span></div><div class="line" id="181749"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#181749" label="[18:17:49]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Achieving 6502 or maybe 80386-level performance is the &quot;easy&quot; part - going from there to something with Intel Core 2-level performance is the more problematic task</span></div><div class="line" id="182317"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182317" label="[18:23:17]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i mean there's like 7 orders of magnitute between 80386 and core 2 transistor counts</span></div><div class="line" id="183344"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#183344" label="[18:33:44]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.meetup.com/guix-london/events/303448758/">https://www.meetup.com/guix-london/events/303448758/</a>  &lt;--- i'm talking about the bootstrapping effort for riscv in guix london in 30 mins</span></div><div class="line" id="190622"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#190622" label="[19:06:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: and it's not desirable to do it for modern monstrosities of what's sold ax x86/64 nowadays</span></div><div class="line" id="190710"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#190710" label="[19:07:10]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">64bit in general seem a little unrealistic, because an intermediate FPGA deployment is either expensive or not feasible</span></div><div class="line" id="190826"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#190826" label="[19:08:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a 32bit x86 however, this would be great if such existed, fully opensource with permissive license, without relying on proprietary tools</span></div><div class="line" id="190933"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#190933" label="[19:09:33]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am pretty sure 32bit x86 patents have expired</span></div><div class="line" id="190942"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#190942" label="[19:09:42]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so sounds like that should be perfectly doable</span></div><div class="line" id="210702"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#210702" label="[21:07:02]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">IIRC the original patents on AMD64 also expired in 2021</span></div><div class="line" id="211649"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#211649" label="[21:16:49]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you could even create an x64 chip</span></div><div class="line" id="211651"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#211651" label="[21:16:51]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nice</span></div><div class="line" id="211655"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#211655" label="[21:16:55]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that even include sse2 i think</span></div><div class="line" id="215543"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#215543" label="[21:55:43]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, SSE2 definitely was on the market in 2004 already</span></div><div class="line" id="215548"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#215548" label="[21:55:48]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so it should be expired too</span></div><div class="line" id="215652"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#215652" label="[21:56:52]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sse2 is part of the x64 spec</span></div><div class="line" id="215659"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#215659" label="[21:56:59]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can't really have x86_64 without it</span></div><div class="line" id="215705"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#215705" label="[21:57:05]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am just not sure which version of sse it was</span></div><br /></div></body></html>